# AI, Model, Tooling, and Automation Rules

Do not hardcode model behavior, tool behavior, domain behavior, or workflow behavior unless explicitly required.

Prefer capability-based routing:

```text
task need
→ required capability
→ available model/tool/connector
→ policy check
→ execution
→ result validation
→ provenance logging
```

## Model Call Rules

For model calls, track where applicable:

- model/provider
- model version
- input references
- output references
- prompt/config version
- cost/usage
- confidence/quality
- limitations

Do not present model output as verified truth.

## Embedding Rules

When creating embeddings, store:

- embedding model
- embedding model version
- embedding dimension
- content hash
- source/object ID
- chunking strategy
- created timestamp
- index name

Do not re-embed unchanged content.

Support reindexing when model changes.

## Prediction Rules

Predictions must:

- be tied to an evidence gap or task objective
- identify model/tool used
- include model/tool version
- include input provenance
- include uncertainty/confidence when possible
- include limitations
- not be presented as proof

Never label a generic LLM guess as a specialized model prediction.

## Tool Execution Rules

Never auto-run:

- unknown tools
- unknown scripts
- unknown packages
- unknown GitHub repositories
- unverified binaries
- arbitrary remote code

Use approved tool registries and explicit policy checks.

Log:
- inputs
- outputs
- artifacts
- errors
- tool version
- execution environment
